# Runtime Cognitive Coalition Formation on Reconfigurable Physical Compute Substrates

## A Revised Theoretical Framework (v0.4)

**Subtitle:** Non-Linear Cost Coupling, Temporal Task Structure, and Refined Causal Closure

ouadi maakoul and SI TEAM .

## Abstract

We present v0.4 of a formal framework for studying runtime cognitive coalition formation on physically constrained, reconfigurable compute substrates. The central scientific question remains narrow and falsifiable: *Does runtime role reassignment on a physically reconfigurable substrate produce a measurable advantage over fixed-role dynamic routing, under equal resource budgets?* This revision incorporates validated refinements from the literature. The principal advances are: (i) a non-linear reconfiguration–routing coupling \(C_{\text{extra}}(\nu_R) = C_{\text{base}} + \eta \nu_R^\kappa\) with \(\kappa \ge 1\), replacing the linear cost model; (ii) an explicit decomposition of the switching cost \(c_{\text{sw}} = \lambda_1 \tau_{\text{reconf}} + \lambda_2 E_{\text{reconf}} + \lambda_3 D_{\text{state\_migration}}\), connecting hardware metrics to the switching threshold; (iii) a bi-directional isolation index \(\Gamma_{\text{full}}(S)\) that distinguishes internal integration, external leakage, and external driving; (iv) a Markov switching task model that captures temporal correlations beyond Poisson arrivals; (v) a real options formulation of the reconfiguration decision, providing an upper bound on the myopic marginal-gain policy; and (vi) a strengthened comparison against offline-optimized static routing (MoE-style) and exploitation of locality in coalition structure generation. The framework maintains the core decomposition: **Adaptation Advantage** \(\rightarrow\) **Switching Cost** \(\rightarrow\) **Phase Boundary**. We propose a micro-world experiment with systematically varied parameters as the immediate next scientific step.

