## 3. Physical Compute Substrate

### 3.1 Graph Model

At time \(t\), the substrate is

\[
G_t = (V, E_t, Q_t, \mathcal{H}_t)
\]

where:

- \(V\) is a finite set of physical compute nodes.
- \(E_t\) is the set of active communication links.
- \(Q_t = [q_{ir}(h_i)]\) is the capability matrix.
- \(\mathcal{H}_t = \{h_i(t)\}_{i \in V}\) are physical/configuration states.

### 3.2 Capability Vector

Each node \(i\) has a capability vector

\[
q_i = (q_i^{\text{compute}}, q_i^{\text{memory}}, q_i^{\text{bandwidth}}, q_i^{\text{energy}}, q_i^{\text{locality}}, q_i^{\text{reconfig}}).
\]

Its effective capability for role \(r\) under configuration \(h_i\) is \(q_{ir}(h_i)\).

### 3.3 Physical Cost (Dimensionally Consistent)

For nodes \(i, j\), communication cost is

\[
c_{ij} = \alpha \tilde{d}(i,j) + \beta \tilde{b}_{ij}^{-1} + \gamma \tilde{\ell}_{ij}
\]

where \(\tilde{d}(i,j)\), \(\tilde{b}_{ij}^{-1}\), and \(\tilde{\ell}_{ij}\) are normalized (dimensionless) distance, inverse bandwidth, and switching latency, respectively. The coefficients \(\alpha, \beta, \gamma\) carry the appropriate units so that \(c_{ij}\) is expressed in a common cost unit.

