## 5. Role Fluidity

### 5.1 Roles and Configurations

Let \(\mathcal{R}\) be a finite set of cognitive roles. Each node \(i\) has configuration \(h_i \in \mathcal{H}_i\) and role \(r_i(t) \in \mathcal{R}\).

Role fluidity permits

\[
r_i(t_1) \neq r_i(t_2)
\]

without replacing node \(i\).

### 5.2 Hardware-Mediated Role Differentiation

A role is not merely a software label. Reconfiguration changes the family of functions the node can execute:

\[
\mathcal{F}_i(h_a) \neq \mathcal{F}_i(h_b).
\]

For some roles \(r_a \neq r_b\),

\[
q_{ir_a}(h_a) > q_{ir_a}(h_b), \qquad q_{ir_b}(h_b) > q_{ir_b}(h_a).
\]

We refer to this mechanism as **Hardware-Mediated Role Fluidity (HMRF)**.

### 5.3 Context Restoration and State Migration

For physical substrates such as partial FPGA reconfiguration or dynamic weight loading in distributed neural accelerators, switching roles incurs not only reconfiguration latency \(\tau_{\text{reconf}}\) but also state-migration and cache-invalidation costs. Recent work on runtime-reconfigurable RISC-V ISA extensions demonstrates that seamless state handover during reconfiguration requires a dedicated memory handover manager, and that software fallback overhead can reach \(1289.93\%\) when full semantic transparency is required. This motivates the explicit decomposition of the switching cost:

\[
c_{\text{sw}} = \lambda_1 \tau_{\text{reconf}} + \lambda_2 E_{\text{reconf}} + \lambda_3 D_{\text{state\_migration}},
\]

where \(E_{\text{reconf}}\) is the energy cost of reconfiguration, \(D_{\text{state\_migration}}\) is the distance or cost of migrating internal state, and \(\lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3\) convert to a common cost unit.

### 5.4 Role-Fluidity Hypothesis

Define the role-fluidity gain:

\[
\Delta_{\text{RF}} = R_{\text{fix}} - C_{\text{reconf}}.
\]

The hypothesis is \(\Delta_{\text{RF}} > 0\) in at least one identifiable regime.

