## 6. Cost Model and Adaptation

### 6.1 Task Sequence and Temporal Correlations

Let \(T = (t_1, \ldots, t_n)\) be a task sequence drawn from a distribution \(P_T\). We extend the model beyond Poisson arrivals to a Markov switching process with persistence parameter \(\rho \in [0,1]\), where \(\rho\) controls the autocorrelation of task types. The task-switching rate is

\[
\nu_T = \frac{\text{number of task changes}}{\text{unit time}}.
\]

However, not every task change triggers a reconfiguration. Define the **role-reconfiguration rate**:

\[
\nu_R = \frac{\text{number of role reconfigurations}}{\text{unit time}}.
\]

Then:

\[
\nu_R \leq \nu_T.
\]

### 6.2 Costs

Let \(c_{\text{sw}}\) be the average cost of a role switch, decomposed as above. Then

\[
C_{\text{switch}} = \nu_R c_{\text{sw}}.
\]

Define extra costs:

\[
C_{\text{extra}}(\nu_R) = C_{\text{base}} + \eta \nu_R^\kappa,
\]

where \(C_{\text{base}}\) captures baseline routing and synchronization overhead independent of reconfiguration, and \(\eta \nu_R^\kappa\) with \(\kappa \ge 1\) captures the non-linear degradation of routing tables and cache coherence at high reconfiguration rates. This replaces the linear model and yields a convex phase boundary.

### 6.3 Marginal-Gain Switching Policy

The controller observes the current task \(t\) and estimates the gain from reconfiguring:

\[
G_t = U(\theta_{\text{new}}, t) - U(\theta_{\text{current}}, t).
\]

Reconfigure if:

\[
G_t > c_{\text{sw}}^{\text{effective}}.
\]

This transforms CRG-2 into a genuine decision policy:

\[
\Pi_2: \text{observe} \rightarrow \text{estimate gain} \rightarrow \text{decide} \rightarrow \text{reconfigure or retain}.
\]

This policy is myopic: it ignores the option value of waiting. A real options formulation would treat the reconfiguration decision as an optimal stopping problem with switching costs. The value function satisfies a Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation, and the optimal strategy can take qualitatively different forms depending on the problem data. The myopic policy provides a lower bound; the real options policy provides an upper bound on achievable performance.

