## 7. Theoretical Results

### 7.1 Assumptions

**A1 — Task heterogeneity.** There exist tasks \(t_a, t_b\) with

\[
\Theta^*(t_a) \neq \Theta^*(t_b)
\]

and both tasks have non-zero probability under \(P_T\).

**A2 — Hardware-mediated role differentiation.** There exists a node \(i\) and configurations \(h_a, h_b\) such that

\[
\mathcal{F}_i(h_a) \neq \mathcal{F}_i(h_b)
\]

and for some \(r_a \neq r_b\),

\[
q_{ir_a}(h_a) > q_{ir_a}(h_b), \qquad q_{ir_b}(h_b) > q_{ir_b}(h_a).
\]

**A3 — Resource scarcity.** Under budget \(B\), no fixed assignment \(\Theta_{\text{fix}}\) achieves the task-optimal utility for all tasks in \(\{t_a, t_b\}\).

**A4 — Feasible reconfiguration.** \(\tau_{\text{reconf}} \leq \tau_{\text{task}}\).

### 7.2 Theorem A (Adaptation Advantage)

**Theorem A.** Define

\[
U_{\text{adapt}} = \mathbb{E}_{t \sim P_T} \left[ \max_{\theta \in \Theta} U(\theta, t) \right]
\]

and

\[
U_{\text{fix}} = \max_{\theta \in \Theta} \mathbb{E}_{t \sim P_T} \left[ U(\theta, t) \right].
\]

Then:

\[
\Delta U_{\text{adapt}} = U_{\text{adapt}} - U_{\text{fix}} \geq 0
\]

with equality if and only if there exists a single configuration \(\theta^*\) that is optimal for all tasks with positive probability.

**Proof.** By the fundamental inequality

\[
\mathbb{E}[\max_\theta U(\theta, t)] \geq \max_\theta \mathbb{E}[U(\theta, t)],
\]

we immediately have \(\Delta U_{\text{adapt}} \geq 0\). For strict inequality, suppose two configurations \(\theta_a, \theta_b\) satisfy

\[
P(t_a) > 0, \qquad P(t_b) > 0
\]

and

\[
U(\theta_a, t_a) - U(\theta_b, t_a) \geq \delta_a > 0,
\]

\[
U(\theta_b, t_b) - U(\theta_a, t_b) \geq \delta_b > 0.
\]

If no single configuration can simultaneously eliminate both losses, then \(\Delta U_{\text{adapt}} > 0\). \(\square\)

**Corollary (Regret Interpretation).** Define the regret of configuration \(\theta\) for task \(t\):

\[
R(\theta, t) = U^*(t) - U(\theta, t)
\]

where \(U^*(t) = \max_{\theta'} U(\theta', t)\). Then

\[
R_{\text{fix}} = \min_\theta \mathbb{E}_{t \sim P_T}[R(\theta, t)]
\]

and

\[
\Delta U_{\text{adapt}} = R_{\text{fix}}.
\]

The adaptation advantage is literally the regret imposed by the obligation to maintain a fixed configuration.

### 7.3 Theorem B (Switching Threshold with Non-Linear Costs)

**Theorem B.** Under A1–A4, if

\[
R_{\text{fix}} > C_{\text{base}},
\]

then there exists a critical reconfiguration rate \(\nu_c > 0\) solving

\[
R_{\text{fix}} = C_{\text{base}} + \eta \nu_c^\kappa + \nu_c c_{\text{sw}},
\]

such that for any task sequence with \(\nu_R < \nu_c\), there exists a feasible CRG-2 policy \(\Pi^*\) satisfying

\[
\mathbb{E}_T \left[ J(\text{CRG-2}, T; \Pi^*) \right] > \mathbb{E}_T \left[ J(\text{CRG-1}, T) \right]
\]

under equal resource budgets.

**Proof.** Construct \(\Pi^*\) using the marginal-gain switching policy: reconfigure when \(G_t > c_{\text{sw}}^{\text{effective}}\). The net expected utility difference satisfies

\[
J_{\text{CRG-2}} - J_{\text{CRG-1}} \geq R_{\text{fix}} - \nu_R c_{\text{sw}} - C_{\text{base}} - \eta \nu_R^\kappa.
\]

For \(\nu_R < \nu_c\), the right-hand side is positive. Hence CRG-2 strictly dominates CRG-1 in expectation. \(\square\)

### 7.4 Corollaries

- If \(\nu_R \to \infty\), then \(\nu_R c_{\text{sw}} + \eta \nu_R^\kappa \to \infty\) and role fluidity becomes too costly.
- If \(\nu_R \to 0\), reconfiguration is well amortized.
- If \(B \to B_{\text{abundant}}\), A3 fails and CRG-1 can duplicate roles.
- If nodes are homogeneous, A2 fails and hardware-mediated role fluidity loses its advantage.
- If \(\nu_R = 0\) (no reconfiguration), CRG-2 reduces to CRG-1.
- If \(\kappa = 1\) and \(\eta = 0\), the linear model is recovered.
- If \(\kappa > 1\), the phase boundary is convex, and the critical rate \(\nu_c\) is lower than in the linear model for the same \(C_{\text{base}}\).

