## 8. Analytical Phase Boundary for Two-Task Micro-World

### 8.1 Setup

Consider two configurations:

\[
\theta_A, \theta_B
\]

and two tasks:

\[
t_A, t_B
\]

with probabilities:

\[
P(t_A) = p, \qquad P(t_B) = 1 - p.
\]

Suppose:

\[
U(\theta_A, t_A) = a,
\]

\[
U(\theta_B, t_A) = a - \delta_A,
\]

\[
U(\theta_B, t_B) = b,
\]

\[
U(\theta_A, t_B) = b - \delta_B,
\]

with \(\delta_A, \delta_B > 0\).

### 8.2 Adaptation Advantage

The adaptive system achieves:

\[
U_{\text{adapt}} = p a + (1 - p) b.
\]

The two fixed configurations give:

\[
U_A = p a + (1 - p)(b - \delta_B) = p a + (1 - p)b - (1 - p)\delta_B,
\]

\[
U_B = p(a - \delta_A) + (1 - p)b = p a + (1 - p)b - p\delta_A.
\]

Therefore:

\[
U_{\text{fix}} = \max(U_A, U_B) = p a + (1 - p)b - \min(p\delta_A, (1 - p)\delta_B).
\]

Hence:

\[
\boxed{
\Delta U_{\text{adapt}} = \min(p\delta_A, (1 - p)\delta_B).
}
\]

This is strictly positive if \(p > 0\), \(1 - p > 0\), \(\delta_A > 0\), \(\delta_B > 0\).

### 8.3 Threshold with Non-Linear Costs

The switching threshold becomes the unique positive solution to:

\[
\boxed{
\min(p\delta_A, (1 - p)\delta_B) = C_{\text{base}} + \eta \nu_c^\kappa + \nu_c c_{\text{sw}}.
}
\]

For \(\kappa = 1\), this yields the linear form:

\[
\nu_c = \frac{\min(p\delta_A, (1 - p)\delta_B) - C_{\text{base}}}{c_{\text{sw}} + \eta}.
\]

For \(\kappa > 1\), the phase boundary is convex, and \(\nu_c\) is lower than in the linear case.

### 8.4 Phase Function

Define:

\[
\Phi = R_{\text{fix}} - C_{\text{base}} - \eta \nu_R^\kappa - \nu_R c_{\text{sw}}.
\]

Then:

\[
\Phi > 0 \Rightarrow \text{role reconfiguration has positive net value}
\]

\[
\Phi < 0 \Rightarrow \text{fixed-role dynamic routing is sufficient}
\]

\[
\Phi = 0 \Rightarrow \text{phase boundary}.
\]

The full phase boundary is:

\[
\boxed{
\Phi(H_{\text{hardware}}, H_{\text{task}}, R, B, \nu_R, c_{\text{sw}}, \kappa, \eta) = 0.
}
\]

