## 9. Hardware Heterogeneity Measure

### 9.1 Corrected Definition

Let

\[
Q = [q_{ir}]
\]

be the capability matrix. Define:

\[
H_{\text{hardware}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{R}|} \sum_{r \in \mathcal{R}} \frac{\sigma_i(q_{ir})}{\mu_i(q_{ir}) + \epsilon}
\]

where \(\sigma_i(q_{ir})\) is the standard deviation of capabilities for role \(r\) across nodes, and \(\mu_i(q_{ir})\) is the mean. This is the average coefficient of variation, measuring dispersion of capability.

### 9.2 Structural Diversity

A more complete measure incorporates the correlation structure of capability profiles:

\[
H_{\text{hardware}}^{\text{full}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{R}|} \sum_r \frac{\sigma_i(q_{ir})}{\mu_i(q_{ir}) + \epsilon} + \kappa \, H_{\text{corr}}(Q)
\]

where \(H_{\text{corr}}(Q)\) characterizes diversity of capability profiles, defined as the average pairwise correlation between capability vectors of different nodes. Two nodes with the same average capability but different profiles are treated as more heterogeneous under \(H_{\text{corr}}\) than under the coefficient of variation alone.

