## Appendix C: Proof of Theorem B (Switching Threshold with Non-Linear Costs)

**Theorem B.** Under A1–A4, if
\[
R_{\text{fix}} > C_{\text{base}},
\]
then there exists a critical reconfiguration rate \(\nu_c > 0\) solving
\[
R_{\text{fix}} = C_{\text{base}} + \eta \nu_c^\kappa + \nu_c c_{\text{sw}},
\]
such that for any task sequence with \(\nu_R < \nu_c\), there exists a feasible CRG-2 policy \(\Pi^*\) satisfying
\[
\mathbb{E}_T \left[ J(\text{CRG-2}, T; \Pi^*) \right] > \mathbb{E}_T \left[ J(\text{CRG-1}, T) \right]
\]
under equal resource budgets.

**Proof.** Construct \(\Pi^*\) as the marginal-gain switching policy: at each task change, estimate
\[
G_t = U(\theta_{\text{new}}, t) - U(\theta_{\text{current}}, t).
\]
Reconfigure if \(G_t > c_{\text{sw}}^{\text{effective}}\), where \(c_{\text{sw}}^{\text{effective}}\) is chosen to account for the effective switching cost. The net expected utility difference between CRG-2 under \(\Pi^*\) and CRG-1 satisfies
\[
J_{\text{CRG-2}} - J_{\text{CRG-1}} \geq R_{\text{fix}} - \nu_R c_{\text{sw}} - C_{\text{extra}}(\nu_R),
\]
where \(C_{\text{extra}}(\nu_R) = C_{\text{base}} + \eta \nu_R^\kappa\). This follows because the adaptive policy can always choose to reconfigure only when the gain exceeds the switching cost, capturing at least \(R_{\text{fix}}\) in expected utility, while incurring switching costs at rate \(\nu_R c_{\text{sw}}\) and extra overhead \(C_{\text{extra}}(\nu_R)\).

Define
\[
f(\nu) = R_{\text{fix}} - C_{\text{base}} - \eta \nu^\kappa - \nu c_{\text{sw}}.
\]
We have \(f(0) = R_{\text{fix}} - C_{\text{base}} > 0\) by assumption. Also \(f(\nu) \to -\infty\) as \(\nu \to \infty\) since \(\eta > 0, \kappa \ge 1\). Moreover, \(f\) is continuous and strictly decreasing for \(\nu \ge 0\) because
\[
f'(\nu) = -\eta \kappa \nu^{\kappa-1} - c_{\text{sw}} < 0.
\]
Therefore, there exists a unique \(\nu_c > 0\) such that \(f(\nu_c) = 0\), i.e.,
\[
R_{\text{fix}} = C_{\text{base}} + \eta \nu_c^\kappa + \nu_c c_{\text{sw}}.
\]
For any \(\nu_R < \nu_c\), we have \(f(\nu_R) > 0\), so
\[
J_{\text{CRG-2}} - J_{\text{CRG-1}} \geq f(\nu_R) > 0.
\]
Thus CRG-2 strictly dominates CRG-1 in expectation. \(\square\)

**Corollary (Linear Case).** If \(\kappa = 1\) and \(\eta = 0\), then
\[
\nu_c = \frac{R_{\text{fix}} - C_{\text{base}}}{c_{\text{sw}}}.
\]
If \(\kappa > 1\), the phase boundary is convex, and \(\nu_c\) is lower than in the linear case for the same \(C_{\text{base}}\).

