## Appendix D: Analytical Phase Boundary for Two-Task Micro-World

Consider two configurations \(\theta_A, \theta_B\) and two tasks \(t_A, t_B\) with probabilities
\[
P(t_A) = p, \quad P(t_B) = 1-p.
\]
Suppose
\[
U(\theta_A, t_A) = a, \quad U(\theta_B, t_A) = a - \delta_A,
\]
\[
U(\theta_B, t_B) = b, \quad U(\theta_A, t_B) = b - \delta_B,
\]
with \(\delta_A, \delta_B > 0\).

**Adaptation advantage.** The adaptive system achieves
\[
U_{\text{adapt}} = p a + (1-p) b.
\]
The two fixed configurations give
\[
U_A = p a + (1-p)(b - \delta_B) = p a + (1-p)b - (1-p)\delta_B,
\]
\[
U_B = p(a - \delta_A) + (1-p)b = p a + (1-p)b - p\delta_A.
\]
Therefore
\[
U_{\text{fix}} = \max(U_A, U_B) = p a + (1-p)b - \min(p\delta_A, (1-p)\delta_B).
\]
Hence
\[
\Delta U_{\text{adapt}} = \min(p\delta_A, (1-p)\delta_B).
\]
This is strictly positive if \(p \in (0,1)\), \(\delta_A > 0\), \(\delta_B > 0\).

**Threshold.** The switching threshold is the unique positive solution to
\[
\min(p\delta_A, (1-p)\delta_B) = C_{\text{base}} + \eta \nu_c^\kappa + \nu_c c_{\text{sw}}.
\]
For \(\kappa = 1\), this yields
\[
\nu_c = \frac{\min(p\delta_A, (1-p)\delta_B) - C_{\text{base}}}{c_{\text{sw}} + \eta}.
\]
For \(\kappa > 1\), the phase boundary is convex and \(\nu_c\) is lower.

**Phase function.** Define
\[
\Phi = R_{\text{fix}} - C_{\text{base}} - \eta \nu_R^\kappa - \nu_R c_{\text{sw}}.
\]
Then \(\Phi > 0\) indicates role reconfiguration has positive net value; \(\Phi < 0\) indicates fixed-role dynamic routing is sufficient; \(\Phi = 0\) is the phase boundary. The full phase boundary is
\[
\Phi(H_{\text{hardware}}, H_{\text{task}}, R, B, \nu_R, c_{\text{sw}}, \kappa, \eta) = 0.
\]

