## Appendix E: Causal Closure and Bi-directional Isolation Index

**Causal influence.** For deterministic systems, define the horizon-dependent causal influence from node \(i\) to node \(j\) as
\[
D_{i \to j}^{(\tau)} = \mathbb{E}_{x, x', \xi} \left[ d\left( X_j(t+\tau; do(X_i=x), \xi), \; X_j(t+\tau; do(X_i=x'), \xi) \right) \right],
\]
where \(x, x'\) are drawn from a distribution over interventions, \(\xi\) represents stochastic noise, and \(\tau\) is a time horizon. Let \(W_{ij}^{(\tau)} = D_{i \to j}^{(\tau)}\). The matrix \(W^{(\tau)}\) is directed.

**Internal integration, external leakage, external driving.**
\[
W_{\text{in}}(S) = \sum_{i,j \in S} W_{ij}^{(\tau)},
\]
\[
W_{\text{out}}(S) = \sum_{i \in S, j \notin S} W_{ij}^{(\tau)},
\]
\[
W_{\text{ext} \to S}(S) = \sum_{i \notin S, j \in S} W_{ij}^{(\tau)}.
\]

**Bi-directional isolation index.**
\[
\Gamma_{\text{full}}(S) = \frac{W_{\text{in}}(S)}{W_{\text{in}}(S) + \omega_1 W_{\text{out}}(S) + \omega_2 W_{\text{ext} \to S}(S) + \epsilon},
\]
with \(\omega_1, \omega_2 > 0\). Require \(\Gamma_{\text{full}}(S) \geq \gamma_{\min}\).

**Properties.** Since all terms are non-negative and \(\epsilon > 0\), \(\Gamma_{\text{full}} \in [0,1]\). If \(W_{\text{out}} = W_{\text{ext} \to S} = 0\), then \(\Gamma_{\text{full}} \to 1\) as \(\epsilon \to 0\), indicating a perfectly isolated coalition. If external driving dominates, \(\Gamma_{\text{full}}\) decreases, penalizing externally driven pipelines. The weights \(\omega_1, \omega_2\) allow calibration: a high \(\omega_2\) penalizes externally driven coalitions more heavily, distinguishing self-governing cognitive coalitions from peripheral processing nodes.

