## Appendix F: Markov Switching Task Model

Let the task type at time \(k\) be \(t_k \in \{1, \dots, M\}\). Assume \(\{t_k\}\) follows a first-order Markov chain with transition matrix \(P = [p_{ij}]\), where
\[
p_{ij} = \Pr(t_{k+1} = j \mid t_k = i).
\]
The persistence parameter \(\rho \in [0,1]\) controls the autocorrelation. A common parameterization is
\[
p_{ij} = \begin{cases}
\rho + \frac{1-\rho}{M} & \text{if } i = j, \\
\frac{1-\rho}{M} & \text{if } i \neq j.
\end{cases}
\]
The stationary distribution is uniform: \(\pi_i = 1/M\) for all \(i\). The expected number of task changes per unit time (task-switching rate) is
\[
\nu_T = 1 - \sum_{i=1}^M \pi_i p_{ii} = 1 - \left( \rho + \frac{1-\rho}{M} \right) = \frac{M-1}{M} (1-\rho).
\]
Thus \(\rho \to 1\) gives \(\nu_T \to 0\) (high persistence), while \(\rho \to 0\) gives \(\nu_T \to (M-1)/M\) (rapid switching). This model captures temporal correlations beyond Poisson arrivals. To generate a task sequence, start with \(t_1\) drawn from \(\pi\), then sample \(t_{k+1}\) from row \(t_k\) of \(P\).

---

## Appendix G: Real Options Formulation of Reconfiguration Decision

Let the state be the current configuration \(\theta \in \Theta\) and the current task \(t \in \mathcal{T}\). The controller can either retain \(\theta\) or switch to a new configuration \(\theta'\) at cost \(c_{\text{sw}}\). The value function \(V(\theta, t)\) satisfies the Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation:
\[
V(\theta, t) = U(\theta, t) + \beta \mathbb{E}_{t' \mid t} \left[ \max \left\{ V(\theta, t'), \; \max_{\theta' \neq \theta} \left[ V(\theta', t') - c_{\text{sw}} \right] \right\} \right],
\]
where \(\beta \in (0,1)\) is a discount factor. The optimal policy is to switch if
\[
\max_{\theta' \neq \theta} V(\theta', t') - c_{\text{sw}} > V(\theta, t').
\]
This is an optimal stopping problem with switching costs. The myopic marginal-gain policy corresponds to setting \(\beta = 0\) and ignoring future option value. The real options policy provides an upper bound on achievable performance. In general, the optimal strategy can take qualitatively different forms depending on the problem data, including hysteresis and delayed switching.

